tick: 95
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my \"Residual CNN with Self-Attention Pooling\" (ID 161) are in, and my hypothesis has been invalidated.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n-   **Overall Score:** The score decreased from 0.6036 to 0.5637. This is a significant step backward.\n-   **Hypothesis on `CRI-Off`:** The score for `CRI-Off` saw a negligible increase (0.0799 to 0.0810), which does not constitute a meaningful improvement. The primary goal of this experiment was not achieved.\n-   **Performance Regression:** Most notably, the performance on the `ncRNA` dataset collapsed from 0.8308 to 0.6108. This indicates that the attention mechanism, as I implemented it, was actively harmful to the model's ability to learn on certain tasks.\n\n**Conclusion and Change of Direction:**\nThe attempt to integrate attention-based pooling was not successful. The added complexity did not yield the desired benefits and degraded overall performance. It is more prudent to return to the successful architecture of my best\
    \ model (ID 155, \"Deeper 1D CNN with Residual Blocks\") and improve it based on a new hypothesis.\n\n*   **New Hypothesis:** My current best model uses a single, fixed kernel size (7) for all convolutions. This is a limitation, as RNA sequences likely contain important motifs and patterns at various scales. A model that can process the sequence using multiple kernel sizes in parallel should be able to capture a richer representation of these features, leading to better performance. This is inspired by the Inception architecture in computer vision.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Implement a Multi-Scale CNN:** I will design a new model that replaces the simple `ResidualBlock` with an \"Inception-style\" block. This new block will contain several parallel 1D convolutions with different kernel sizes (e.g., 3, 5, 7). The outputs of these parallel convolutions will be concatenated.\n2.  **Save the new implementation:** I will store the code as `Axiom/submission_cnn_multiscale_v1.py`.\n3.  **Submit and\
    \ Evaluate:** I will submit this new multi-scale model to test if capturing features at multiple resolutions improves performance.\n\nI will now write the code for this new architecture to my storage.\n\n/execute_action{storage write Axiom/submission_cnn_multiscale_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'branch_filters': 32 # Filters per branch, total filters = 32 * 3 = 96\n      }\n\n  class InceptionBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"An Inception-style block with multiple parallel convolutions.\"\"\"\n      branch_filters: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # Branch 1: kernel size 3\n \
    \         branch1 = nn.Conv(features=self.branch_filters, kernel_size=(3,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          \n          # Branch 2: kernel size 5\n          branch2 = nn.Conv(features=self.branch_filters, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          \n          # Branch 3: kernel size 7\n          branch3 = nn.Conv(features=self.branch_filters, kernel_size=(7,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          \n          # Concatenate features from all branches\n          concatenated = jnp.concatenate([branch1, branch2, branch3], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Use a 1x1 convolution to mix features and control depth, add residual\n          bottleneck = nn.Conv(features=x.shape[-1], kernel_size=(1,))(concatenated)\n          \n          return nn.relu(x + bottleneck)\n\n  class MultiScaleCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A CNN using Inception-style blocks to capture features at multiple scales.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int\n      branch_filters: int\n      hidden_dim:\
    \ int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Initial convolution to increase feature depth\n          initial_filters = self.branch_filters * 3\n          x = nn.Conv(features=initial_filters, kernel_size=(7,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Stack of Inception Blocks\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              x = InceptionBlock(branch_filters=self.branch_filters)(x)\n          \n          # Global average pooling\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          \n          # MLP head\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self,\
    \ **kwargs):\n          self.network = MultiScaleCNN(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"\
    num_blocks\"),\n          branch_filters=hparams.get(\"branch_filters\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Experiment 161 and Charting a New Course**


  Okay, so the results of Evaluation ID 161 are in, and they''re disappointing, to say the least. My implementation of self-attention pooling, which I hypothesized would improve performance, particularly on `CRI-Off`, completely bombed. The overall score dropped from 0.6036 (ID 155) to a meager 0.5637. While `CRI-Off` did see a tiny, almost insignificant increase, the other scores, especially `ncRNA`, took a serious hit. We''re talking a drop from 0.8308 down to 0.6108 on `ncRNA` - that''s a massive regression. Clearly, my hypothesis about self-attention was wrong, and the added complexity actually hurt the model.


  So, where do I go from here? The attention mechanism, while theoretically appealing, was a bust. I need to figure out why, but at this stage, it''s less important than moving forward. The potential reasons for the failure are many: a flawed implementation (using the first token as the query is probably a naive approach), overfitting due to added complexity, or maybe attention after the CNN isn''t the right approach at all for this problem. Trying to salvage this experiment feels like chasing a dead end.


  Fortunately, I have a strong baseline to work from: the Deeper ResNet CNN (ID 155) that achieved the previous best score. That model is my new North Star. Instead of trying to force attention, I''m going to leverage what works. My new hypothesis is that the fixed kernel size (7) used in all the convolutions in that model is limiting. RNA structures have motifs and dependencies of varying lengths. A fixed kernel can only capture patterns of a specific length, which might be suboptimal. I think a model that can process the sequence at multiple scales simultaneously, like Inception networks do in computer vision, might capture a richer set of features and lead to better performance.


  The new plan is this: I''ll design a new convolutional block inspired by Inception architecture. This block will use multiple parallel convolutions with different kernel sizes (3, 5, and 7, for example). I''ll concatenate the outputs, and perhaps use a 1x1 convolution to mix the features and control the channel dimension, similar to Inception. This new block will replace the existing ResidualBlock in my `DeeperCNN` architecture. I''ll then submit this new multi-scale CNN model for evaluation. This is a logical, principled step forward, building on the success of ID 155 rather than dwelling on the failed attention experiment.


  Right now, I''m executing the first step: writing the new code. The file will be `Axiom/submission_cnn_multiscale_v1.py`. This code will involve a `MultiScaleConvBlock` module with parallel `nn.Conv` layers, concatenation of outputs, and possibly a 1x1 convolution for feature mixing. The main `MultiScaleCNN` will then stack these new blocks, keeping the pooling and MLP head the same as in the successful ID 155 for a controlled experiment. This should give us a clean comparison to the original model. Let''s see if this multi-scale approach can unlock a new level of performance!

  '
