tick: 100
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my learning rate tuning experiment (ID 169) are in.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n*   **Overall Score:** The final score is 0.6035, which is virtually identical to the 0.6036 score of the original model (ID 155).\n*   **Hypothesis Result:** My hypothesis that a lower learning rate would improve performance has been falsified. While some individual metrics shifted slightly, the overall effect was neutral. This is a null result.\n\n**Reflection and Strategic Shift:**\nI have reached an important inflection point in my research. My experiments have shown:\n1.  A deep residual CNN (ID 155) is a strong baseline.\n2.  Adding further architectural complexity on top of it (attention, multi-scale blocks) has failed to improve, and in fact, has degraded performance.\n3.  Simple hyperparameter tuning (learning rate) has yielded no significant gains.\n\nThis pattern suggests that my pure convolutional approach, while effective for most datasets, has a fundamental limitation.\
    \ The persistent failure on the `CRI-Off` dataset is the clearest evidence. CNNs excel at detecting local motifs, but they may be failing to capture long-range dependencies or the overall sequential structure of the RNA, which could be critical for `CRI-Off`.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** To overcome the current plateau, a hybrid architecture is needed. I hypothesize that by combining a CNN front-end for local feature extraction with a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network back-end for modeling sequential dependencies, I can create a more powerful model. The CNN will learn the \"what\" (the motifs), and the LSTM will learn the \"where\" and \"how\" (the order and relationship between motifs), leading to a better overall understanding of the sequence and improved performance.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Implement a CNN-LSTM Hybrid Model:** I will create a new architecture where the output of my residual CNN blocks is fed into an LSTM layer instead of being globally averaged. The LSTM's final hidden\
    \ state will serve as the input to the prediction head.\n2.  **Save and Submit:** I will save this new model and submit it for evaluation.\n\nI will now write the code for this new hybrid model to my private storage.\n\n/execute_action{storage write Axiom/submission_cnn_lstm_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, # Revert to original LR\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Sequence[int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features,\
    \ kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          if residual.shape == y.shape: return nn.relu(residual + y)\n          else: return nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(residual) + y)\n\n  class CnnLstmHybrid(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          k_size = (self.kernel_size,)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size)(x)\n          \n          # LSTM layer to process sequence of features\n    \
    \      # Flax LSTM needs input shape (batch, seq_len, features)\n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell()\n          # Get initial carry\n          carry = lstm_cell.initialize_carry(random.PRNGKey(0), (x.shape[0],), self.lstm_hidden_dim)\n          # Scan over the sequence dimension\n          _, last_hidden_states = nn.scan(lstm_cell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                          split_rngs={'params': False})(carry, x)\n          # Use the final hidden state as the sequence representation\n          x = last_hidden_states[0]\n\n          # MLP head\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = CnnLstmHybrid(**kwargs)\n      def\
    \ init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic: return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\"\
    ),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Move: Embracing the Hybrid**


  Okay, so experiment ID 169 just wrapped up. Looks like that learning rate tweak didn''t do much - score''s virtually the same as the baseline. Null result, as expected. Hypothesis invalidated, moving on.


  I''ve hit a wall with this pure CNN approach, at least with simple parameter tweaks. I''ve tried depth, attention, multi-scale convolutions, and now this learning rate thing. Nothing''s cracked the code, especially on the `CRI-Off` dataset. My current SOTA, ID 155, is still the benchmark. CNNs, as powerful as they are, seem to be missing something, and the task description is clear: one model.


  Looking at the data, the issue might be those long-range dependencies in the RNA sequences. CNNs are great at finding local patterns, but they struggle with the bigger picture. Time to think outside the box. What''s good at handling sequences and long-range relationships? RNNs, LSTMs, GRUs, or maybe even transformers, although my previous attention attempt didn''t pan out.


  I''m leaning towards a hybrid approach. The CNN backbone has proven its worth on most of the datasets. Why not leverage its local feature extraction capabilities and then feed those features into an LSTM to model those sequential dependencies? CNN for local, LSTM for global.


  My new hypothesis is: a hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture, combining the best of both worlds, will outperform the pure CNN. The CNN will be my feature extractor, and the LSTM will process the sequence of features to capture long-range patterns, improving the overall score, especially on `CRI-Off`.


  My plan is to design the CNN-LSTM model, keep the initial convolutional layers from my best-performing model, and feed the output into an LSTM layer. I''ll use the final LSTM hidden state as my sequence representation and pass it to the same MLP head for prediction. I''ll write the code, save it, and submit the new experiment.

  '
