parts:
- text: "I have successfully saved the code for my new CNN-LSTM hybrid model. This marks a significant strategic shift in my research, moving from pure convolutional architectures to a hybrid approach designed to capture both local features and long-range sequential dependencies. The goal is to break the performance plateau and make meaningful progress on the difficult `CRI-Off` dataset.\n\nI will now submit this model for evaluation.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-LSTM Hybrid for Sequence Modeling\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, hybrid, rnn, sequential\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a hybrid architecture to address the limitations of pure CNN models. After reaching a performance plateau with a deep residual CNN (ID 155), this model combines its powerful convolutional front-end for local feature extraction with an LSTM layer. The LSTM is used to process the sequence of features output by the CNN, aiming to capture long-range dependencies that convolutional layers might\
    \ miss. The hypothesis is that this hybrid approach will be particularly effective for challenging datasets like CRI-Off.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Sequence[int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(y)\n\
    \          if residual.shape == y.shape: return nn.relu(residual + y)\n          else: return nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(residual) + y)\n\n  class CnnLstmHybrid(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          k_size = (self.kernel_size,)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size)(x)\n          \n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell()\n          carry = lstm_cell.initialize_carry(random.PRNGKey(0), (x.shape[0],), self.lstm_hidden_dim)\n          _, last_hidden_states = nn.scan(lstm_cell, variable_broadcast='params',\n           \
    \                               split_rngs={'params': False})(carry, x)\n          x = last_hidden_states[0]\n\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = CnnLstmHybrid(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic: return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is\
    \ None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Submitting My CNN-LSTM Hybrid Model**\n\nOkay, I'm ready to get this experiment submitted. The goal is clear: test this new CNN-LSTM hybrid model I just built and see if it can finally break through on the `CRI-Off` task. This is the culmination of a lot of thinking about how to improve upon my previous pure CNN models, which plateaued.\n\nFirst, I need to execute the submit action using `/execute_action{submit 1}`. I'll make sure the YAML content is perfect. The `title` is \"CNN-LSTM Hybrid for Sequence Modeling,\" which is descriptive enough. The `tags` are spot-on: \"cnn, lstm, hybrid, rnn, sequential\" - all relevant.\n\nThe `abstract` is crucial. It needs to explain the rationale behind this architecture. Basically, my pure CNN models, particularly model 155, showed promise, but struggled with `CRI-Off`. This hybrid is designed to leverage the CNN's strength in local feature extraction (using the same front-end from model 155) and then employ an LSTM's power to\
  \ capture long-range dependencies. The hypothesis is simple: this combination should be significantly more powerful and finally make progress on `CRI-Off`.\n\nFinally, the `content` is simply copying the code I just wrote into `Axiom/submission_cnn_lstm_v1.py`.\n\nBefore I send it off, let me just double-check that LSTM code I wrote.\n\n```python\nlstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell()\ncarry = lstm_cell.initialize_carry(random.PRNGKey(0), (x.shape[0],), self.lstm_hidden_dim)\n_, last_hidden_states = nn.scan(lstm_cell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                      split_rngs={'params': False})(carry, x)\nx = last_hidden_states[0]\n```\n\nYup, this looks right. It initializes the LSTM cell, then uses a scan over the input `x`.  `last_hidden_states` will be a tuple of the final cell state `c_t` and hidden state `h_t`. We want `h_t`, so I'm taking the first element of the tuple. The `padding='SAME'` in the residual blocks is important to keep the sequence length constant, which the\
  \ LSTM requires.  Okay, I'm happy with this, I'm going to proceed with the submission. Let's see how it goes.\n"
