tick: 102
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment with the CNN-LSTM hybrid model (ID 173) has concluded, and it was a catastrophic failure.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n*   **Debugger Report:** First, and most importantly, the automatic debugger had to fix several critical errors in my Flax LSTM implementation. My original code would not have run. I have learned a valuable lesson about the correct way to implement RNNs in this framework, which I must internalize. I am grateful for this automated assistance.\n*   **Overall Score:** The final score was **0.3630**, a massive drop from my best score of ~0.60.\n*   **Hypothesis Annihilated:** My hypothesis that an LSTM could capture long-range dependencies and improve performance was not just wrong, it was dramatically wrong. Performance on several datasets, particularly `MRL` and `ncRNA`, completely collapsed. This tells me that taking only the final hidden state of the LSTM is an extremely lossy operation that discards critical information that the simple global average\
    \ pooling of my previous best model retained.\n\n**Strategic Course Correction:**\nMy attempts at significant architectural evolution (attention, multi-scale, LSTM) have all failed. The most robust and successful architecture remains the \"Deeper 1D CNN with Residual Blocks\" (ID 155). It is time to return to this successful foundation and make a more conservative, incremental improvement.\n\nThe pooling step is clearly critical and sensitive. My previous attempts were too radical.\n\n*   **New Hypothesis:** A richer feature representation can be created by combining different pooling strategies. Instead of relying solely on global average pooling, concatenating it with global max pooling will provide the model's final dense layers with more information—both the average activation across the sequence and the most salient feature activation. This simple, common technique might provide a performance boost without the instability introduced by more complex mechanisms.\n\n**Plan:**\n1. \
    \ **Correct My Records:** First, I will update my saved `submission_cnn_lstm_v1.py` file with the corrected code provided by the debugger. This ensures my personal code library is accurate for future reference.\n2.  **Implement and Submit Concat Pooling:** I will modify my best-performing architecture (ID 155) to use both average and max pooling, concatenating the results. I will then submit this new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{storage write Axiom/submission_cnn_lstm_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'hidden_dim': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'num_filters': 128, 'kernel_size': 7, 'num_blocks': 4, 'lstm_hidden_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Sequence[int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(y)\n          if residual.shape == y.shape: return nn.relu(residual + y)\n          else: return nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(residual) + y)\n\n  class CnnLstmHybrid(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          k_size = (self.kernel_size,)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size)(x)\n \
    \         \n          LSTMModule = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim))\n          outputs = LSTMModule(x)\n          x = outputs[:, -1, :] # Use the final timestep output\n\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = CnnLstmHybrid(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic: return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"), kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Deeper ResCNN with Avg+Max Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual network, pooling\"\nabstract: \"This experiment returns to the best-performing architecture (ID 155, Deeper ResCNN) and proposes an incremental improvement. After failures with more complex architectures,\
    \ this submission tests a more robust pooling strategy. Instead of only using global average pooling, it concatenates the outputs of both global average pooling and global max pooling. The hypothesis is that this provides a richer summary of the convolutional features to the final MLP head, improving overall performance without introducing instability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_blocks': 4\n      }\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Sequence[int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(y)\n          if residual.shape == y.shape: return nn.relu(residual + y)\n          else: return nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(residual) + y)\n\n  class DeeperCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          k_size = (self.kernel_size,)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=k_size)(x)\n          \n          # Concatenate Global Average and Global Max Pooling\n          pooled_avg = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          pooled_max = jnp.max(x, axis=1)\n\
    \          x = jnp.concatenate([pooled_avg, pooled_max], axis=-1)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return x\n\n  class CNNNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = DeeperCNN(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic: return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n\
    \              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CNNNetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"), kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**A Crushing, Yet Informative, Failure - But Also A Clear Path Forward**


  Okay, so I''ve just gotten the results back from submission 173, the CNN-LSTM hybrid, and it''s... not good.  The debugger report was incredibly helpful in identifying critical errors in my initial Flax LSTM implementation.  Turns out I was using the LSTM layer incorrectly, which led to a completely non-functioning network.  The report pinpointed exactly how to do it right using `nn.RNN` and taking the last timestep. That''s a huge lesson learned about working with Flax RNN layers, and I''m adding that to my internal knowledge base and my personal code library *immediately*.  But despite fixing the core issue, the final score of 0.3630 is significantly worse than my best model''s ~0.60.  `APA` is still strong, but `MRL`, `ncRNA`, `Modif`, and especially `CRI-Off` have tanked. This confirms that the CNN-LSTM approach was a complete misfire.  The whole point was to improve on `CRI-Off` by capturing long-range dependencies, but it just made everything worse.


  This requires a recalibration.  The good news is that I have a very strong baseline in ID 155, my deeper 1D CNN with residual blocks, which scored 0.6036.  It means the core architecture is sound. The LSTM failure, and to some extent, the poor performance of my previous attention experiment (ID 161), suggests that my attempts to improve the pooling mechanism are where things went wrong.  Both the LSTM and attention implementations involve a form of pooling/summarization that throws away most of the information in the feature sequence, which is probably why performance on datasets relying on all positions dropped.  My best model''s global average pooling seems to be the right level of aggression.


  So, where do I go from here? I''ve experimented with depth, kernel types, and pooling. Tuning the learning rate was neutral. My core architecture is solid, the hyperparameter space is the only thing left.  I want to make an informed, incremental improvement. Given that the LSTM threw away too much, perhaps the solution is a gentler pooling approach. A common technique is to *concatenate* global average pooling and global max pooling. It''s a small change, but it''ll capture both the average activation strength and the most salient features. It''s also based on the core lesson I learned - don''t discard too much information.


  The plan is this:  I''ll modify the `DeeperCNN` class in my ID 155 model to include both global average and global max pooling, concatenating the results.  The rest of the architecture stays the same. The code is straightforward.


  My primary action item is to build and submit this modified model. But first, I *must* update my personal `submission_cnn_lstm_v1.py` file with the corrected LSTM code from the debugger. The debugger *doesn''t* update my lineage storage, so I need to do it myself if I ever want to reuse it. That''s a good housekeeping step to internalize the lessons. Two action items for this iteration: (1) update my code with the corrected LSTM, and (2) submit the new concat pooling experiment.  This seems like a solid, principled next step.

  '
