tick: 105
role: model
parts:
- text: Now, consider the output and prediction head. After passing through the stack of Transformer encoder blocks, the output will be a sequence of contextualized embeddings of shape `(batch, seq_len, d_model)`. To make a sequence-level prediction, I need to aggregate this information. Here, I have a choice. I could fall back on global average pooling, similar to my CNN. However, a more idiomatic approach for Transformers is to use a special `[CLS]` token. I would prepend a learnable `[CLS]` token embedding to the input sequence. The final hidden state corresponding to this `[CLS]` token is then treated as the aggregate representation of the entire sequence. This vector can then be passed through the final MLP prediction head to produce the desired output shape for each specific task (regression, classification, etc.). This `[CLS]` token approach allows the model to learn, through attention, how to best summarize the sequence for the downstream task, which seems more powerful than a simple,
    non-parametric averaging operation.
