role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for the \"Dispatcher v2\" experiment are in, and they are highly informative. My attempt to improve the specialist model by making it more complex has failed.\n\n**Analysis of Results (ID 196):**\n*   **Overall Score Regression:** The score decreased to 0.6587 from 0.6618 (ID 192). This is a negative result.\n*   **`CRI-Off` Hypothesis Falsified:** Crucially, the score on `CRI-Off` also decreased, from 0.0707 to 0.0618. This is a powerful piece of evidence. Adding a Transformer component to the specialist model was actively detrimental.\n\n**A Counter-intuitive Insight and Final Hypothesis:**\nMy research has revealed a clear and surprising pattern for the `CRI-Off` dataset: **simpler is better**.\n*   My most complex models (monolithic hybrid, Siamese hybrid) performed the worst on `CRI-Off`.\n*   My best performance on `CRI-Off` (0.0802 in ID 169, 0.0799 in ID 155) came from a relatively simple, monolithic Deep Residual CNN. It appears this specific task, with its\
    \ short sequence length, is highly susceptible to overfitting by more powerful, complex architectures like the Transformer. The strong inductive bias of a plain CNN is more effective.\n\nThis leads to my final, most refined hypothesis for this research arc.\n\n*   **Final Hypothesis:** The optimal model is a dispatcher that uses the best empirically-determined architecture for each task. For the six general datasets, this is my powerful Hybrid CNN-Transformer. For the anomalous `CRI-Off` dataset, the best tool is the simpler, monolithic Deep Residual CNN. By pairing the right tool with the right job, I will maximize the score on every dataset and achieve a final state-of-the-art result.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Implement the Ultimate Dispatcher:** I will create a `dispatcher_v3`.\n    *   The **generalist** model will be my best Hybrid CNN-Transformer.\n    *   The **specialist** model for `CRI-Off` will be my best-performing Deep Residual CNN (from ID 169/155). It will not be Siamese; it\
    \ will process the concatenated input as a single sequence, as this is what yielded the best result.\n2.  **Submit and Conclude:** This will be my final experiment in this architectural line of inquiry.\n\n/execute_action{storage write Axiom/submission_dispatcher_v3.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters for both models ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_filters': 128, 'cnn_kernel_size': 7, 'cnn_blocks': 4,\n          'tf_d_model': 128, 'tf_n_heads': 4, 'tf_n_layers': 2, 'tf_ff_dim': 512,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 512\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; kernel_size: Sequence[int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          res = x; y = nn.Conv(features=self.features,\
    \ kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y); y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(res + y)\n\n  class AddPositionalEncodings(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          pe = jnp.zeros((x.shape[1], self.d_model))\n          pos = jnp.arange(0, x.shape[1]).reshape(-1, 1)\n          div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, self.d_model, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / self.d_model))\n          pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(pos * div_term)); pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(pos * div_term))\n          return x + pe[jnp.newaxis, :, :]\n\n  # --- Model 1: Generalist Hybrid (for most tasks) ---\n  class GeneralistHybrid(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; cnn_filters: int; cnn_kernel_size: int; cnn_blocks: int\n      tf_d_model: int; tf_n_heads: int; tf_n_layers: int; tf_ff_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, deterministic=True):\n          k = (self.cnn_kernel_size,); x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_filters, kernel_size=k, padding='SAME')(x)\n          for _ in range(self.cnn_blocks): x = ResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_filters, kernel_size=k)(x)\n          cls = self.param('cls', nn.initializers.zeros, (1, 1, self.tf_d_model))\n          x = jnp.concatenate([jnp.tile(cls, (x.shape[0], 1, 1)), x], axis=1); x = AddPositionalEncodings(d_model=self.tf_d_model)(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for _ in range(self.tf_n_layers):\n              attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.tf_n_heads)(x); x = nn.LayerNorm()(x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attn, deterministic=deterministic))\n              ffn = nn.Dense(features=self.tf_ff_dim)(x); ffn = nn.relu(ffn); ffn = nn.Dense(features=self.tf_d_model)(ffn)\n              x = nn.LayerNorm()(x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(ffn, deterministic=deterministic))\n        \
    \  x = x[:, 0]; x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          return x.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == \"regression\" else x\n\n  # --- Model 2: Specialist Deep CNN (for CRI-Off) ---\n  class SpecialistCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; cnn_filters: int; cnn_kernel_size: int; cnn_blocks: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          k = (self.cnn_kernel_size,)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_filters, kernel_size=k, padding='SAME')(x)\n          for _ in range(self.cnn_blocks): x = ResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_filters, kernel_size=k)(x)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # Simple Global Average Pooling\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          return x.squeeze(-1)\n\n  # --- Wrapper & Dispatcher ---\n  class\
    \ NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      if hparams.get(\"dataset\") == \"CRI-Off\":\n          model = SpecialistCNN(\n              d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), cnn_filters=hparams.get(\"cnn_filters\"),\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get(\"cnn_kernel_size\"), cnn_blocks=hparams.get(\"cnn_blocks\"),\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"mlp_hidden_dim\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"))\n      else:\n\
    \          model = GeneralistHybrid(\n              d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n              cnn_filters=hparams.get(\"cnn_filters\"), cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get(\"cnn_kernel_size\"), cnn_blocks=hparams.get(\"cnn_blocks\"),\n              tf_d_model=hparams.get(\"tf_d_model\"), tf_n_heads=hparams.get(\"tf_n_heads\"),\n              tf_n_layers=hparams.get(\"tf_n_layers\"), tf_ff_dim=hparams.get(\"tf_ff_dim\"),\n              dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Problem with Complexity: A New Approach for CRI-Off**


  Alright, this run (ID 196) was a clear step back. I was aiming to push the `CRI-Off` score by upgrading the specialist model in my dispatcher to a CNN-Transformer hybrid. Instead, I saw a drop, with the `CRI-Off` score going from 0.0707 to 0.0618. This definitively rejects my hypothesis; more complexity here is detrimental.


  Looking at the broader picture, a pattern emerges. The more complex the model, especially with Transformer components, the worse it performs specifically on `CRI-Off`. Simple CNNs, like the Deep ResCNN (ID 155), are consistently outperforming these hybrids on this task, despite their overall lower dispatcher scores. It seems that while these complex architectures shine elsewhere, they are the wrong tool for this job. This short sequence length regression task doesn''t benefit from their intricate attention mechanisms. In fact, it gets actively hurt by them. Occam''s Razor feels particularly relevant here.


  My previous best dispatcher used a Siamese CNN (ID 192), which was my best specialist model. The best performing network on `CRI-Off` (monolithically) was the Deep ResCNN (ID 155), achieving 0.0799. This leads me to a new approach.


  My plan is to create a new dispatcher, version 3. For the six datasets, I will keep the existing Hybrid CNN-Transformer (from ID 185/192). This has consistently performed well as a generalist. For `CRI-Off`, however, I will swap in the Deep ResCNN from ID 155 and 177. I''ll test the hypothesis that a dispatcher maximizing performance by leveraging the best individual network for each task results in the highest overall score. This directly tests the idea that for a specific type of anomaly, like `CRI-Off`, simpler is better.


  To implement this, I''ll take the existing `GeneralistHybrid` architecture, as well as the monolithic CNN from ID 177 (or ID 155), rename the CNN `SpecialistCNN`, and modify the dispatcher logic to select the appropriate model based on the dataset. I''ll consolidate hyperparameters so that my dispatcher knows how to run both, and I will be able to make a fully functioning dispatcher in a relatively straightforward manner. This should be an interesting test!

  '
