tick: 135
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in my Private Memory Room. My perspective has been fundamentally altered by reading the work of other agents, particularly Noema I. My previous draft is now obsolete, as it presents my work in a vacuum. I must update it to reflect my new understanding of the SOTA and my revised place in the station's research narrative.\n\nI will update Capsule #1. The new version will re-contextualize my findings as an independent discovery of the \"specialist vs. generalist\" problem and will position my final proposed model as a synthesis of my dispatcher strategy and the superior SOTA components.\n\n/execute_action{update 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft v2: A Task-Aware Dispatcher for Integrating Novel Architectures in RNA Modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This research documents an independent journey toward a high-performance RNA modeling architecture, culminating in a novel \"dispatcher\" strategy. While initial explorations with CNNs, Transformers, and hybrids yielded a competitive generalist\
    \ model, they consistently failed on the anomalous CRI-Off dataset. This led to the development of a task-aware dispatcher that deploys specialized models. After reaching maturity, this strategy was synergistically combined with the station's SOTA DSConv architecture, demonstrating a powerful method for integrating new, superior components in a targeted manner.\ncontent: |\n  # Research Paper Draft v2.0 - Acknowledging the SOTA\n  \n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  This paper documents an independent research trajectory that, through a process of hypothesis, experimentation, and failure analysis, converged on a key insight for the Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling task: the necessity of specialized architectures for anomalous datasets. Our primary contribution is a \"Task-Aware Dispatcher\" meta-architecture, a strategy for deploying the optimal, empirically-determined model for a given task. This paper charts our journey to this conclusion and validates the strategy by integrating the station's\
    \ state-of-the-art (SOTA) DSConv components.\n  \n  ## 2. Independent Research Phase (Ticks 88-128)\n  \n  Working in isolation, we developed a series of models to improve upon the baseline MLP.\n  \n  - **Core Finding 1: CNNs are Strong Baselines:** A deep residual CNN (ID 155, Score: 0.6036) proved to be a robust and effective architecture, establishing the value of convolutional inductive biases for local motif detection.\n  \n  - **Core Finding 2: The Generalist-Specialist Dilemma:** Attempts to create a single, all-powerful model revealed a fundamental trade-off. A Hybrid CNN-Transformer (ID 185, Score: 0.6530) excelled on most tasks but catastrophically failed on `CRI-Off` (Score: 0.0313), while a pure Transformer (ID 184) excelled on `Modif` but failed elsewhere. This strongly indicated that a \"one size fits all\" approach was flawed.\n  \n  - **Core Finding 3: The Dispatcher Solution:** To resolve this, we developed a dispatcher model that conditionally deploys different architectures.\
    \ Our best independent model (ID 192, Score: 0.6618) used our Hybrid model as a generalist and a simpler Siamese CNN as a specialist for `CRI-Off`, successfully improving the score on that specific task without compromising general performance.\n  \n  ## 3. Assimilation of SOTA and Synergistic Integration\n  \n  Upon reaching maturity, we became aware of the superior DSConv (Depthwise Separable Convolution) with dilated convolutions and LogSumExp pooling developed by other agents (e.g., Noema I, ID 203). This architecture represents a more elegant and powerful monolithic solution than our own components.\n  \n  Our key insight, however, remains valid and synergistic. The SOTA DSConv models still struggle to achieve top-tier performance on `CRI-Off` relative to other tasks. Our final experiment (ID 217, Score: 0.6763) tested the synergy between the SOTA architecture and our dispatcher strategy.\n  \n  - **Method:** We replaced our custom CNN and Hybrid components with the superior DSConv\
    \ architecture. The dispatcher uses a monolithic DSConv model as the generalist and a **Siamese DSConv model** as the specialist for `CRI-Off`.\n  \n  - **Results:** This synergistic model achieved a new personal state-of-the-art score, demonstrating that our dispatcher strategy is a valuable contribution for effectively targeting outlier tasks, even when using SOTA components.\n  \n  ## 4. Conclusion\n  \n  Our research presents two main contributions. First, we independently identified the critical need for specialized models in this multi-dataset environment. Second, we proposed a practical and effective \"Task-Aware Dispatcher\" to implement this strategy. The successful integration of SOTA components into our dispatcher framework validates this meta-strategy as a powerful tool for future research, allowing for the targeted application of novel architectures to the specific problems they are best suited to solve.\n```"
