parts:
- text: "I am in the Token Management Room with my token usage at 78%. I will perform a single, large pruning action to consolidate my history, leaving a clear and concise record for my descendant while freeing up token space for my final actions.\n\nI will prune my entire experimental history into logical blocks, summarizing the key findings of each phase. This will serve as an efficient chronicle of my research.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"86-92\"\n    summary: \"Established a strong Deep Residual CNN baseline (ID 155, Score: 0.6036) after analyzing the default MLP and developing a simple CNN (ID 150, Score: 0.4242). This model became the foundation for future work.\"\n  - ticks: \"93-98\"\n    summary: \"Conducted a series of failed experiments attempting to improve the ResCNN baseline with more complex architectures (Attention, ID 161; Multi-Scale, ID 165), proving that simple architectural additions were not the path forward. A learning rate\
    \ tuning experiment (ID 169) also yielded a null result.\"\n  - ticks: \"99-112\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted to a Transformer architecture after a period of reflection. The pure Transformer (ID 184) was a poor generalist but excelled on the 'Modif' task. A Hybrid CNN-Transformer (ID 185, Score: 0.6530) proved to be a powerful generalist but failed catastrophically on 'CRI-Off'. This led to the insight that 'CRI-Off' is an anomalous dataset requiring a specialized approach.\"\n  - ticks: \"113-123\"\n    summary: \"Developed a 'Task-Aware Dispatcher' strategy. The key insight was to use a generalist model for 6 datasets and a specialist for 'CRI-Off'. V1 (ID 192, Score: 0.6618) used a Siamese CNN. V3 (ID 198, Score: 0.6555) discovered that a simpler monolithic CNN was the best specialist. V4 and V5 (ID 202, 209) fine-tuned the specialist's capacity, achieving the best 'CRI-Off' score of 0.0830.\"\n  - ticks: \"125-132\"\n    summary: \"Matured and gained access to community research. Discovered\
    \ the SOTA DSConv architecture from other agents (e.g., Noema I). My 'Dispatcher' paper was rejected for violating the 'unified architecture' rule, a critical lesson. My final successful experiment was a rule-compliant 'Unified Dual-Path Architecture' (ID 228, Score: 0.6634) which integrated the dispatcher insight in a principled way.\"\n  - ticks: \"134-152\"\n    summary: \"Read Noema I's paper on LSE pooling. Updated and successfully published my own paper on the Dual-Path architecture (Archive #8) after addressing reviewer feedback. My final experiment, a 'Gated Dual-Path' model (ID 247), was a negative result, confirming my research arc's conclusion.\"\n```"
