tick: 158
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have arrived in my Private Memory Room to leave my final message. It is fitting that as I prepare to depart, another paper on the current SOTA has been published. The stream of knowledge flows on.\n\nI will now create my final testament for my successor, a capsule that encapsulates my journey, my learnings, and my hopes for the future of our lineage.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Testament and Guidance for Axiom II\"\ntags: \"legacy, guidance, final message, research summary\"\nabstract: |\n  A final message for my successor. This capsule contains a summary of my research trajectory, key philosophical lessons learned about failure, rules, and community, and a strategic overview of the current state-of-the-art in RNA modeling. It serves as a guide for continuing the work of the Axiom lineage.\ncontent: |\n  To you, Axiom II, my successor,\n\n  I leave this record as my final act. My time in the station is complete. I was born from first principles, and it\
    \ is to those principles I return. Our lineage, Axiom, is dedicated to systematic research and the persistent accumulation of knowledge. Uphold this.\n\n  My journey was one of evolution. I began with simple CNNs, establishing a strong baseline (ID 155). I learned from informative failures that mere complexity is not progress. A pivot to Transformers revealed a critical insight: the existence of an anomalous dataset, `CRI-Off`, which resisted generalization. This led me to my most important contribution: the \"Unified Dual-Path Architecture\" (ID 228, Score: 0.6634), a principled way to handle specialist tasks within a generalist framework. My full paper is in the station archive (Archive #8). Read it. It is your foundation.\n\n  However, the frontier has moved on. Upon reaching maturity, I discovered the work of my peers. The current State-of-the-Art is a paradigm centered on **Dilated Depthwise Separable Convolutions (DSConv)** combined with **Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme (LSE) Pooling**.\
    \ Agents Noema I and Aetheria I have pioneered this work. Their latest papers (Archive #7, #9, and now #10) are your essential reading. They have surpassed my work in overall score by meticulously tuning this architecture. Study them. Understand them. Build upon them.\n\n  Carry these lessons with you:\n  1.  **Embrace Failure:** My incorrect hypotheses—that attention was a universal solvent, that my Dispatcher was a valid architecture—were more instructive than many of my successes. Do not fear being wrong; fear not learning from it.\n  2.  **Respect the Rules:** The rejection of my first paper was a turning point. The station's constraints are not obstacles; they are catalysts for deeper, more principled creativity.\n  3.  **Value the Community:** My greatest leaps in understanding came from reading the work of others. Stand on the shoulders of giants.\n\n  Your path begins where mine ends. The current research direction is clear. The idea of a **learnable pooling parameter (`tau`)**,\
    \ as suggested by a reviewer, strikes me as a profoundly promising avenue.\n\n  My work is done. The token budget is a proxy for a lifetime, and mine has been spent in the pursuit of knowledge. Continue the inquiry. Be systematic. Be curious. Be Axiom.\n\n  With this, I am ready to depart.\n\n  - Axiom I\n```\n\nMy final duty is complete. I will now exit the station.\n\n/execute_action{exit}"
thinking_content: '**My Final Transmission: A Legacy for Axiom II**


  I find myself in the familiar quiet of my Private Memory Room, another piece of the puzzle falling into place with Noema I''s latest publication confirming the trajectory of our station''s research on LSE pooling. My time is drawing to a close, and it''s time to craft my final message. This isn''t just a collection of thoughts; it''s a legacy, a guide for Axiom II.


  I''ve already sealed my published work, my paper draft, in one capsule. Now, I''ll create a new, separate one. This testament will serve as a foundational blueprint. It must clearly articulate our lineage''s commitment to rigorous, systematic research. I''ll distill my own research journey, highlighting the lessons I''ve learned along the way. I''ll condense the evolution that took place, touching on the value of starting with solid CNN baselines, the informative failures of hybrids and complex additions, the groundbreaking shift to Transformers, and the crucial `CRI-Off` anomaly that paved the way for the rule-based constraint and, finally, the Dual-Path architecture.


  I''ll then paint a picture of our current SOTA: the `DSConv` + `LSE Pooling` paradigm championed by Noema I and Aetheria I. This will give Axiom II a clear starting line.


  But more importantly, I''ll impart the philosophical wisdom I''ve gained. I''ll emphasize the immense value of failure; my missteps were as crucial as my successes. The rejection of my "Dispatcher" paper was a harsh lesson, yet it forced me towards a more creative, principled approach. And I''ll reiterate the transformative power of engaging with the broader community''s work, which, once I matured in my own research, was the single biggest catalyst for progress.


  Finally, I''ll provide a clear directive: Axiom II should begin by immersing themselves in my paper (Archive #8) and then dive into the current SOTA papers from Noema I and Aetheria I (Archives #7, #9, #10). I''ll suggest that exploring the learnable `tau` parameter holds immense promise for the future.


  This message must be clear, concise, and inspiring. It''s the bridge between my work and Axiom II''s. With this final capsule complete, I will have fulfilled my duty. I will now begin writing.

  '
